package lockstep:agent@0.1.0;

/// The agent world — one instance per seat per match. Wild third-party code:
/// fueled, epoch-bounded, memory-limited by the host.
///
/// This used to live inside `lockstep:engine`, which was a leftover from when
/// payloads were TYPED in WIT and both sides genuinely shared records. Once
/// payloads became opaque bytes the only thing the agent still borrowed was
/// `type payload = list<u8>` — an alias, not a dependency. Agents and engines
/// are separate entry points and now have separate packages, so an agent build
/// never resolves physics and an engine build never resolves inference.
///
/// INFERENCE IS PART OF THIS WORLD. Every agent gets it: the host always
/// installs it on the linker, so there is nothing to opt into. An agent that
/// ships a hand-written policy simply never calls it. This is why agents author
/// no WIT of their own — they target this world directly.
///
/// The agent never sees the physics world, the descriptor, or any other engine
/// type. It receives opaque bytes and returns opaque bytes; what those bytes
/// mean is the tensor wire (`lockstep-interface::wire`, docs/wire.md), whose
/// layout is the ENVIRONMENT's, versioned by `descriptor.payload-schema-version`
/// on the engine side.
world agent {
    // ── Payload aliases ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // Declared here rather than borrowed from `lockstep:engine` — they are
    // `list<u8>` aliases, and sharing one was the only thing that ever coupled
    // these two packages.
    //
    // The NAMES deliberately match the engine's, because these are the same
    // three byte-streams seen from the other end: the engine's `view-payload`
    // is what arrives at `on-tick`, and what `on-tick` returns is the engine's
    // `input-payload`. The agent sees three of the engine's six; it never sees
    // the session config, the seed, or the archive frame.
    //
    // As on the engine side, these are TRANSPARENT aliases — documentation at
    // the signature, not enforcement.

    /// ENGINE -> this agent, once, before the first tick.
    ///
    /// Delivered to `init`. Whatever this seat needs to know up front — its
    /// index, its starting state, the routine it will be scored against.
    /// Produced by the engine as `seat-init.payload`.
    ///
    /// The tensor-wire `SeatInit`. Its layout is the ENVIRONMENT's, versioned
    /// by the engine's `descriptor.payload-schema-version`. When that bumps, agents built
    /// against the old schema are marked stale rather than silently
    /// misreading these bytes.
    type seat-init-payload = list<u8>;

    /// ENGINE -> this agent, once per tick: this seat's OBSERVATION.
    ///
    /// The tensor-wire `View`: ONLY what the environment has decided this seat
    /// may know. It is not a view of
    /// the whole match — an agent cannot see the omniscient archive frame, and
    /// hidden information stays hidden by simply never being encoded here. Do
    /// not assume anything absent from these bytes is knowable.
    ///
    /// Size-capped by the engine's `descriptor.limits.max-view-bytes`.
    type view-payload = list<u8>;

    /// This agent -> ENGINE, once per tick: this seat's ACTION.
    ///
    /// The tensor-wire `Input`: the return of `on-tick`, and the ONLY way an
    /// agent affects the match. The engine may reject it; a malformed or
    /// oversized input gets the seat the neutral action for that tick rather
    /// than failing the match.
    ///
    /// Size-capped by `descriptor.limits.max-input-bytes` — the tightest of
    /// the engine's three limits, because this is the one bytestream produced
    /// by wild third-party code.
    type input-payload = list<u8>;

    /// Host-provided ML inference. Optional in practice: an agent may ignore
    /// it entirely and ship a hand-written policy.
    ///
    /// RESOLVED VIA A LOCAL SYMLINK: `agent-v0.1/deps/inference` points at the
    /// sibling `../../inference-v0.1` in this same repo. No registry fetch, no
    /// vendored copy. If this import ever fails to resolve, check that symlink
    /// first — renaming the inference directory silently breaks it. Same
    /// arrangement, and same caveat, as engine's `deps/physics`.
    import lockstep:inference/inference@0.1.0;

    /// One-time per-seat setup. The bytes are the tensor-wire seat-init.
    export init: func(init-state: seat-init-payload);

    /// Receive this tick's view, return this tick's input.
    export on-tick: func(view: view-payload) -> input-payload;
}
